# Security headers for every response. The site is static and loads a
# small, fixed set of third-party origins:
#   - cdn.jsdelivr.net      supabase-js UMD bundle
#   - fonts.googleapis.com  the stylesheet
#   - fonts.gstatic.com     the font files themselves
#   - *.supabase.co         REST, auth and Edge Function calls
# The CSP below allows exactly those and nothing else, so an injected
# <script src> pointing anywhere else simply won't execute.
#
# 'unsafe-inline' is present for script-src because several pages still
# carry inline <script> blocks (marketing-services.html renders its
# catalogue that way) and every page uses inline style attributes.
# Removing it means moving those to files and adding per-request nonces,
# which a static host can't do -- worth revisiting if the site ever
# gains a build step.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), interest-cohort=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: blob:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co wss://*.supabase.co; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'

# HTML: always revalidate with the server, never serve a stale copy from the
# browser cache. This is already Netlify's effective default when no custom
# headers exist, but pinning it explicitly here means it stays correct even
# if that default ever changes.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# CSS/JS: same treatment as HTML -- always revalidate, never serve a
# stale copy straight from the browser cache. These were previously on a
# 1-hour max-age, which just relocated the "stale index.html" bug onto
# CSS/JS instead of fixing it: during active development, a fix could
# ship, get merged, and still appear broken to a tester for up to an hour
# because their browser was silently reusing pre-fix dashboard.js/
# dashboard.css without even checking the server. None of these filenames
# are content-hashed (main.css stays main.css across every deploy), so
# there's no cache-busting alternative short of revalidating on every load.
/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Images: unaffected by this class of bug (never gated a bugfix retest),
# so they keep a longer cache.
/*.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/*.jpeg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
